  <MedicalNote />

  <ArticleImage src="/assets/images/articles/activity-intensity.svg" alt="Illustration of swimming intensity levels and how stroke choice affects calorie burn." />

  <h2 id="by-intensity">How Many Calories Swimming Burns by Intensity</h2>
  <p>Swimming intensity is measured in MET — Metabolic Equivalent of Task. The higher the MET, the more calories you burn per minute. For a 70 kg person:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Swimming Intensity</th><th scope="col">MET</th><th scope="col">Calories per minute</th><th scope="col">Calories per hour</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Leisure / slow</td><td>6.0</td><td>~7.4 kcal</td><td>~440 kcal</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Moderate laps</td><td>7.0</td><td>~8.6 kcal</td><td>~516 kcal</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Vigorous laps</td><td>10.0</td><td>~12.3 kcal</td><td>~735 kcal</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Butterfly / intense</td><td>13.0</td><td>~16.0 kcal</td><td>~960 kcal</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="text-sm text-mute">Heavier swimmers burn more calories at the same MET. Use the <a href="/swimming-calories-calculator/">swimming calories calculator</a> for personalized numbers.</p>

  <h2 id="factors">Factors Affecting Swim Calorie Burn</h2>
  <p>Swimming calorie burn isn't just about strokes. Several variables change your total:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Body weight:</strong> More mass means more energy to move through water. A 90 kg swimmer burns ~20% more calories than a 60 kg swimmer at the same pace.</li>
    <li><strong>Stroke efficiency:</strong> Efficient swimmers glide farther per stroke, slightly reducing burn. Beginners often burn more simply because their technique is less efficient.</li>
    <li><strong>Water temperature:</strong> Cold water raises metabolic rate slightly as your body works to maintain core temperature. Warm water can feel easier, reducing perceived effort.</li>
    <li><strong>Rest intervals:</strong> Short rest between laps keeps heart rate elevated. Long rests lower total calorie burn per session.</li>
    <li><strong>Swim duration:</strong> Longer sessions burn more total calories, but intensity matters more for time-efficient weight loss.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="stroke-comparison">How Each Swimming Stroke Affects Calorie Burn</h2>
  <p>Different strokes target different muscles and demand varying effort:</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Stroke</th><th scope="col">MET</th><th scope="col">Primary Muscles</th><th scope="col">Calories per 30 min (70 kg)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Freestyle</td><td>7.0–10.0</td><td>Full body, shoulders, core</td><td>420–600 kcal</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Backstroke</td><td>7.0–8.0</td><td>Back, glutes, hamstrings</td><td>420–480 kcal</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Breaststroke</td><td>5.0–7.0</td><td>Chest, inner thighs, hips</td><td>300–420 kcal</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Butterfly</td><td>13.0</td><td>Upper body, core, chest</td><td>780+ kcal</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Mix strokes during a session to target different muscles and keep calorie burn high. Use the <a href="/swimming-calories-calculator/">swimming calories calculator</a> to estimate your specific stroke and intensity.</p>

  <h2 id="swim-vs-other">Swimming vs Other Exercises for Weight Loss</h2>
  <p>Swimming competes well with running and cycling for calorie burn, but with key advantages:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Low impact:</strong> Water supports 90% of your body weight, sparing knees, hips, and ankles.</li>
    <li><strong>Full body:</strong> Unlike running (mostly legs), swimming works arms, core, back, and legs simultaneously.</li>
    <li><strong>Sustainable:</strong> Because it's gentle, you can swim more frequently than you might run or cycle at high intensity.</li>
    <li><strong>Comparable burn:</strong> Vigorous swimming (~10.0 MET) is close to running at 6 mph (~9.8 MET) and cycling at 14 mph (~8.0 MET).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Compare swimming directly with other activities using the <a href="/calories-burned-calculator/">calories burned calculator</a>, the <a href="/running-calorie-calculator/">running calorie calculator</a>, and the <a href="/cycling-calories-calculator/">cycling calories calculator</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="maximize">Tips for Maximizing Swim Calorie Burn</h2>
  <p>Get more from every swim session:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Use interval sets:</strong> Sprint 25 meters, rest 15 seconds, repeat. Intervals raise average intensity.</li>
    <li><strong>Mix strokes:</strong> Alternate freestyle, backstroke, and breaststroke to recruit different muscles.</li>
    <li><strong>Reduce rest time:</strong> Keep rest intervals to 10–20 seconds between laps to stay in the calorie-burning zone.</li>
    <li><strong>Add pull sets:</strong> Use a pull buoy to isolate upper-body effort and increase arm engagement.</li>
    <li><strong>Extend gradually:</strong> Add 5 minutes per week to build endurance and total calorie burn.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Pair your swim routine with a daily calorie target from the <a href="/">calorie calculator</a> and a macro plan from the <a href="/macro-calculator/">macro calculator</a> for best results.</p>

